  abstract = {This record describes the method and data structure for two studies evaluating a playful VR rehabilitation game using a VR headset and force-feedback gloves. Study 1 had 7 participants and Study 2 had 14 participants. Participants played the rehabilitation game and completed questionnaires before, during and after play. The underlying anonymised participant-level data consists of CSV files per study, summarised below; the data is available on request. Files and variables (Study 1 and Study 2, mirrored unless noted): DEMO — demographics: internal participant ID, age, gender, education, occupation, and prior experience with VR, force-feedback gloves, rehabilitation, and VR rehabilitation. ATTITUDES1 / ATTITUDES2 — attitude ratings towards the system before (1) and after (2) play (5-point items plus an expected/perceived playfulness item, 1-10). EXPERIENCE1 / EXPERIENCE2 / EXPERIENCE3 — experience ratings for game subtasks 1-3 (engagement, immersion, glove-control ease, motivation, perceived effect of haptic feedback; mostly 5-point items, with one open-text motivation item). SSQ — Simulator Sickness Questionnaire: the 16 standard symptom items (0-3). NASATLX (Study 2 only) — NASA Task Load Index: six subscale ratings (0-100) and pairwise dimension comparisons. index.csv — study metadata and per-tab descriptions. Instruments: bespoke VR rehabilitation game (VR headset + force-feedback gloves); Simulator Sickness Questionnaire (SSQ); NASA Task Load Index (NASA-TLX, Study 2); cust},
}

